# The port vendors two files from The Specialists 2.1 byte for byte:
# bots21.txt and bot_say21.txt. They are Windows files, so they are CRLF,
# and the port's own comment promises "byte for byte".
#
# core.autocrlf is "input", which normalises CRLF to LF when a file is
# added. That silently changed these two files when they were vendored:
# the blob in the repository has LF, while the working copy of anyone who
# vendored them still has CRLF. So a fresh clone builds a port whose
# embedded bot texts are not the upstream bytes -- and a test that checks
# those bytes fails on every machine except the one that vendored them.
#
# -text turns the normalisation off for them, so the bytes in the
# repository are the bytes in every checkout, on every platform.
tools/port/tsp-rs/crates/ts21/data/bots21.txt -text
tools/port/tsp-rs/crates/ts21/data/bot_say21.txt -text

# Our own bot chat file is a CRLF file too: it is the sanitized form of
# the TS one, which is a Windows file, and the reviewed artifact is
# 2215 bytes of CRLF with a known sha256.
#
# Without this, `git add` normalises the CRLF to LF ("CRLF will be
# replaced by LF the next time Git touches it") and the blob in the
# repository is NOT the file that was reviewed — the same drift the
# vendored files above were caught in, and the unit test that pins the
# artifact's sha256 would then fail on every machine except this one.
tools/deploy/config/bot_say.txt -text
